From: Nathanael Hannebert <nathanael.hannebert@vates.tech>
Date: Wed, 01 Jul 2026
Subject: [PATCH] ipu6: guard workerThread completeBuffer() against
 requests whose pending set was cleared

Fixes a recurring wireplumber SIGABRT with this exact backtrace:

  FATAL default request.cpp:111 assertion "ret == 1" failed in completeBuffer()
    libcamera::Request::Private::completeBuffer(FrameBuffer*) +0x1fc
    libcamera::IPU6CameraData::workerThread() +0x176

`Request::Private::completeBuffer()` does `pending_.erase(buffer)` and
asserts the return value is exactly 1 -- i.e. the buffer must have been
in the request's pending set. On the IPU6 pipeline we observed four
instances of this abort over ~5 weeks. The wireplumber process
auto-recovered each time via systemd-user Restart=, but audio blipped
and it was starting to be user-visible.

The race path we believe hits this:

  1. `IPU6CameraData::workerThread` acquires `queueMutex_`, pops a
     Request from `pendingRequests_`, releases the lock.
  2. The worker spends ~50 ms copying HAL frame data into the
     libcamera FrameBuffer with no lock held.
  3. Concurrently, the application or PipeWire path calls
     `PipelineHandler::stop()`. That runs
     `request->_d()->cancel()` on each waiting request, which calls
     `doCancelRequest()` and clears `pending_`. The IPU6 pipeline
     handler's `stopStreaming()` also iterates its own residual
     `pendingRequests_` and calls `buffer->_d()->cancel()` +
     `completeBuffer()` on the buffers there.
  4. If the request the worker popped in (1) shares its buffer
     object with a request that has since been cancelled -- or if
     the same request appears in another cancel path -- the buffer's
     entry in `pending_` may have already been removed.
  5. Worker calls `pipe()->completeBuffer(request, buffer)`.
     `pending_.erase(buffer)` returns 0. Assertion fires. abort.

We haven't fully characterised the exact path (kervel/libcamera fork
is closed to issues; symbols in the wireplumber core dumps only tell
us the worker is the culprit, not which app / stopDevice() timing
triggered it). But regardless of the specific cause, the fix is the
same: never blindly call `completeBuffer()` -- verify the request
still has pending buffers to complete first, and if not, drop the
frame with a warning rather than aborting.

`Request::Private::hasPendingBuffers()` returns true iff the request's
`pending_` set is non-empty. If it's already been cleared (by
cancel()/complete()/anyone else), our completeBuffer() call would
abort; log-and-skip instead.

Residual race window between the guard check and the actual
`pending_.erase(buffer)` is negligible in practice -- the guard is
a couple of atomic-free memory reads whereas the crash window we're
closing is a full frame copy (~50 ms).

--- a/src/libcamera/pipeline/ipu6/ipu6.cpp
+++ b/src/libcamera/pipeline/ipu6/ipu6.cpp
@@ -454,8 +454,29 @@ void IPU6CameraData::workerThread()
 				request->_d()->metadata().set(controls::SensorTimestamp,
 							     dqBuf ? static_cast<int64_t>(dqBuf->timestamp) : 0);

-				pipe()->completeBuffer(request, buffer);
-				pipe()->completeRequest(request);
+				/*
+				 * Guard against completing a buffer whose parent
+				 * Request has already had its `pending_` set
+				 * cleared (e.g. by Request::Private::cancel() on
+				 * a PipelineHandler::stop() / stopDevice() path).
+				 *
+				 * Without this guard,
+				 * Request::Private::completeBuffer() aborts on
+				 *   ASSERT(pending_.erase(buffer) == 1);
+				 * (request.cpp:111), taking wireplumber with it.
+				 *
+				 * See archlinux-ipu6-webcam
+				 * IPU6-investigation-2026-05-22.md gotcha #4 for
+				 * the recurring backtrace this fixes.
+				 */
+				if (!request->_d()->hasPendingBuffers()) {
+					LOG(IPU6, Warning)
+						<< "Dropping stale buffer for cancelled/"
+						   "completed request";
+				} else {
+					pipe()->completeBuffer(request, buffer);
+					pipe()->completeRequest(request);
+				}
 			}
 		}

